{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Discovered while fixing the ubuntu/py3.11 hang (89d5-61da-b621-47f8): the shared\nVerified/branch-CI suite in _build-and-test.yml carries\n`--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread`, but every OTHER CI pytest invocation has NO\nper-test hang guard. `timeout-minutes` (where declared at all) only bounds a whole job,\nso one deadlocked test hides behind the last `-q` dot with no traceback and no culprit\nnamed. None of the affected lanes declares `timeout-minutes`, so today they run under\nGitHub's 360-minute default.\n\n## Scope: every unguarded pytest invocation in .github/workflows\n\nTriage named six; a full sweep of the workflow directory found two more (`_eval-discipline`,\n`_optionality`). All eight are covered here so the class is closed, not half-closed.\n\n## Operator decision applied\n\nThe recorded operator decision (2026-08-10) is: per-lane budgets calibrated from historical\nruntime data, generous allowance for legitimate live-service variance; do NOT copy one blanket\ntimeout across all lanes. Applied as an auditable rule:\n\n**Per-test budget = at least 10x the lane's observed mean per-test duration, floored at 300s.**\n\nDurations were measured from recent successful GitHub Actions runs by reading each pytest\nstep's `started_at`/`completed_at` (runs 30986591446, 30964805133, 31003041092, 30983268038).\n`--timeout-method=thread` is used everywhere, for the same load-bearing reason as the shared\nsuite: the default `signal` method arms SIGALRM in the main thread and cannot fire while a\nworker is blocked in a C-level flock/socket/subprocess call, which is exactly the hang shape\nthese live-service lanes exercise.\n\n| Lane | Observed evidence | Budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| external-integration `external-llm` (live LLM matrix) | 16 tests, step 138-870s (bedrock 347->870s) | 900 |\n| external-integration `external` (live Jira Cloud) | 19 tests, step 85-120s | 600 |\n| external-integration `langfuse-trace` | 1 test, 24-29s | 300 |\n| external-integration `jira-dc-harness` | 63 tests, step 2384-2635s (~41s/test mean) | 900 |\n| structured-output-baseline `structured-matrix` | 1 test driving the whole sweep, 65-173s | 3600 |\n| test.yml `external` | no run history (see below) | 600 |\n| _eval-discipline | offline unit tests | 300 |\n| _optionality degradation-path | offline unit tests | 300 |\n\nTwo lanes have no usable history and are calibrated by analogy, recorded here so the choice is\nauditable rather than invented:\n\n- **test.yml `external`** has NEVER executed: it is gated on a `workflow_dispatch` input that\n  has never been fired, and its credential env keys are all commented out. It is given the same\n  600s as the `external` lane in external-integration.yml, which runs the same `-m external`\n  selection against live Jira.\n- **structured-output-baseline** runs a SINGLE test function that drives the entire sweep, whose\n  size is the dispatch input `max_calls` (default 300 live model calls). A budget sized to the\n  observed 173s would truncate a deliberately larger human-dispatched sweep, so it gets 3600s:\n  20x the worst observation, with headroom for a multiplied call budget.\n\nThe `jira-dc-harness` lane's 900s also clears, by 3x, the in-test field-readiness wait\n(`JIRA_DC_FIELD_READY_TIMEOUT`, default 120s, documented as raisable to 300s in\ntests/external/live_jira_dc/README.md) that runs INSIDE a test and would otherwise be charged\nagainst the per-test budget.\n\n## Dependency availability\n\nNo dependency work is required for seven of eight lanes: `pytest-timeout==2.4.0` is pinned in\nthe `dev` extra (pyproject.toml) and every one of those lanes installs `--extra dev`. The\nexception is `_optionality.yml`'s degradation-path step, which runs in a deliberately minimal\n`/tmp/clean` venv installing only `pytest jsonschema mcp` so that `agents_extra_installed()`\nstays False. `pytest-timeout` is added to that install line rather than carving a permanent\nexemption in the new parity assertion: it is a test-harness tool in the same category as the\n`pytest` already installed there, it is not part of the agents stack whose ABSENCE that lane\nexists to prove, and it is installed in a later step than the clean-core-wheel `find_spec`\nassertions, so what the lane proves is unchanged.\n\n## Anti-regression\n\n`tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py` currently asserts the hang guard only against\n`_build-and-test.yml` (via `_pytest_suite_run_blocks`, which filters on `--dist` and so cannot\nsee a non-xdist lane). It is extended with a sweep over EVERY workflow file in\n.github/workflows, collecting every step whose `run` body invokes pytest in command position,\nand asserting each carries both `--timeout=` and `--timeout-method=thread`. This is what stops\na NEW lane from shipping unguarded; it is deliberately an exhaustive sweep with no allowlist.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `pytest -m \"external and llm_live\"` in external-integration.yml carries\n      `--timeout=900 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC2: `pytest -m \"external and not llm_live\"` in external-integration.yml carries\n      `--timeout=600 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC3: the Langfuse trace step in external-integration.yml carries\n      `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC4: the Jira DC harness step in external-integration.yml carries\n      `--timeout=900 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC5: structured-output-baseline.yml's pytest step carries\n      `--timeout=3600 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC6: test.yml's `pytest -m external` step carries\n      `--timeout=600 --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC7: _eval-discipline.yml and _optionality.yml's pytest steps each carry\n      `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread`, and _optionality.yml's clean venv installs\n      `pytest-timeout` so the flag is honored rather than erroring on an unknown option.\n- [ ] AC8: tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py fails if ANY pytest-invoking step in ANY\n      .github/workflows file lacks either flag (verified by temporarily removing a flag and\n      observing the failure), and passes on the committed tree.\n- [ ] AC9: every rationale above (per-lane budget + its evidence) is recorded as a YAML comment\n      at the step it governs, so the number is auditable at the point of use.\n- [ ] AC10: the workflows remain valid — `make lint` (which runs actionlint) and `make typecheck`\n      pass, and no lane's selection, markers, or behavior changes other than the added flags."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786501967787804001,"uuid":"184a5ea0-9d5b-4390-b3e1-06f29e9cff9f"}